  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing DEM Requirements**\n\nI\'m currently breaking down the requirements for the 3D Geospatial Topographic Relief (DEM) Layer. I\'m focusing on the \"3D Topographic Relief (DEM) Base Layer\" aspect, specifically the seamless integration of an elevation layer and the ability to toggle between 2D and 3D views. This is the central focus of my current thinking.\n\n\n**Visualizing Terrain and Elevation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the \"3D Topographic Terrain View\" aspects. I\'m focusing on the elevation rendering for the five calibration countries and their districts, and I\'ve also incorporated the details of a configurable vertical exaggeration slider. I\'m prioritizing the clear visualization of topographic features, so the elevation color hypsometric tinting scheme is another focus. I\'m also now thinking about interactive camera controls (pitch/tilt, bearing/rotation, zoom, pan, and a view reset option).\n\\n\\n\n\n\n**Developing Referral Pathways**\n\nI\'m now integrating obstetric referral transport routes into the 3D visualization. I\'m focusing on dynamic, color-coded paths based on travel time and feasibility. These routes will navigate the terrain, and I\'ll display the 2D planar and 3D terrain distances, estimated travel time, maximum slope, and elevation profiles upon inspection. Additionally, I\'m thinking about interactive tooltips for health facilities and associated information.\n\n\n**Connecting Terrain and SD**\n\nI\'m now integrating the System Dynamics (SD) model. I\'m focusing on correlating the 3D terrain friction with the `travelTimeHours` parameter to illustrate the connection. User interventions will dynamically update 3D routes to show travel time changes. Discrepancies between the SD model and terrain route travel times will trigger a warning if they exceed 20%.\n\n\n**Calculating Accessibility & Performance**\n\nI\'m now calculating the percentage of each district\'s area with a slope under 10% that\'s accessible to EmONC within a 2-hour timeframe. This data will be integrated into a geo-epidemiological KPI card in the panel. In addition, I\'m focusing on performance optimization. I want smooth WebGL rendering at 30-60 FPS with responsive interaction. There\'s also a high-definition 2D fallback planned. I\'m building a progressive DEM elevation grid generator using accurate elevation data for all 25 districts. I\'m also planning backend API integrations with endpoints for geospatial calculations.\n\n\n**Designing Facility and Terrain**\n\nI\'m now determining package requirements and styling. I need to ensure all required npm packages are included. We have existing packages for styling and motion. For 3D terrain, I\'m thinking of building a WebGL/Canvas 3D interactive Topographic DEM Renderer that addresses perspective projection, terrain meshes, color shaders, routes with terrain clamping, slope analysis, lighting, controls, contour lines, elevation profiles, and 2D/3D toggling. I\'m checking into building a modular 3D GIS system with elevation matrices, slope, travel time algorithms, and topographic accessibility calculations.\n\n\n**Constructing Interactive Components**\n\nI\'m now deeply involved in component design. I\'m focusing on the `Terrain3DCanvas.tsx` component, the interactive WebGL/Canvas rendering engine. I\'m working on the terrain mesh, hypsometric tinting, vertical exaggeration, and shaded relief. I\'m also addressing the 3D routes with dynamic waypoints, facility pins, and controls. Furthermore, I\'m focusing on the `TerrainElevationProfile.tsx` cross-section graph and how it will interactively display altitude, slope, and bottlenecks, as well as the complete overhaul of `GeospatialMapView.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 573712961
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
